<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Pack for a Trip.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Demonstrate comprehension of simple written exchanges in letters, emails, or social media. reading2: Create lists from information read in texts. phonemicAwareness: Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can demonstrate comprehension by identifying steps in written exchanges about trip planning (e.g., 'Please, pack a hat.'). Can locate key information in written checklists for trips (e.g., 'Take a towel.'). Can recognize and decode regularly spelled, one-syllable, or two-syllable words related to beach vocabulary (e.g., /p/ /æk/ in 'pack,' /s/ /ændi/ in 'sandy').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of beach items (e.g., swimsuit, towel, sunscreen, sun hat, flip-flops, beach ball) that were introduced in Lesson 1. The teacher points to each item and asks, "What is this?" and "Do we need this for the beach?" Students respond chorally or individually with one-word answers or simple phrases. For example, "It's a towel. Yes, we need a towel."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences about packing for a trip to the beach, integrating target grammar and vocabulary. The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /b/ in 'beach' and /p/ in 'pack'.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "I am going to the beach this weekend. I am going to pack my bag." (Pointing to bag)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher picks up a flashcard (e.g., swimsuit): "Please, bring your swimsuit for the beach. I always bring my swimsuit."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher picks up another flashcard (e.g., sunscreen): "Please, pack your sunscreen. It is sunny at the beach."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we going to the mountains or the beach?" (Expected: The beach.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I pack a coat or a swimsuit for the beach?" (Expected: A swimsuit.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Trip to the Beach"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated email or a simple written message from a friend inviting to a beach trip, including imperative requests for packing. The text will use vocabulary like 'beach,' 'towel,' 'sunscreen,' 'swimsuit,' 'sun hat,' 'flip-flops,' 'pack,' 'bring,' and 'sunny.' The text will be simple and at an A1.2 reading level, consisting of short sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text (displayed on board or as a handout):</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px; border-radius: 5px;">
                    Hi Leo,<br>
                    We are going to the **beach** this Saturday! It is going to be **sunny** and **fun**.<br>
                    Please **pack** your **swimsuit**. <br>
                    Please **bring** your **towel**.<br>
                    Don't **forget** your **sunscreen** and **sun hat**.<br>
                    We are going to **swim** and **play** in the **sand**.<br>
                    See you!<br>
                    Mia
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Demonstrate comprehension of simple written exchanges about trip planning and identify key information in texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the email aloud first. Students follow along.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is Mia's email about?" (Expected: A trip to the beach / What to pack for the beach.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher reads simple statements based on the text, and students say "True" or "False."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They are going to the beach on Sunday." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia wants Leo to pack a swimsuit." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo needs to forget his sunscreen." (False)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students circle all the items Mia asked Leo to pack in the text. (e.g., swimsuit, towel, sunscreen, sun hat).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading and identifying target vocabulary (beach, towel, sunscreen, swimsuit, sun hat, flip-flops) and grammar (imperatives: "Please pack/bring...", "Don't forget...") in written form. Recognizing and decoding regularly spelled one- or two-syllable words like 'pack,' 'sandy,' 'beach,' 'swim.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/Cloze: "Fill the Bag" Worksheet.</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the email, but with key vocabulary words missing. They read the sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank with beach vocabulary (e.g., 'towel,' 'sunscreen,' 'swimsuit,' 'sun hat'). This reinforces reading for detail and vocabulary recognition.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Please pack your __________." (Word bank: towel, hat, sand, swim)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides a word bank for students to choose from.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled: "Circle the Imperatives."</b> Students read a short text (similar to the email) and identify and circle all the imperative verbs or phrases (e.g., "Please pack," "Bring," "Don't forget"). This helps them recognize instructions in written form.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher leads an example on the board first.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then work individually on their worksheets.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Read and Verify."</b> Students work in pairs. Each student reads their completed sentences from activity 1 (the "Fill the Bag" worksheet) to their partner. The partner listens and checks if the words are correct according to the original email. This encourages active reading and peer correction while reinforcing phonemic awareness when reading words like /p/ /æk/ in 'pack' or /b/ /iːtʃ/ in 'beach'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Trip to the Beach"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format: "My Beach Packing List"</b> Students receive a new, slightly different short written message or a simple note from another character (e.g., 'Grandma') about preparing for a beach trip. This new text will contain 4-5 items to pack, similar to the presentation text, but with some variation in items (e.g., 'flip-flops,' 'bucket'). Students read the message carefully and create their own "Beach Packing List" on a provided template, writing down each item they need to bring. This directly addresses the learning outcome "Can create lists from information read in texts."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students are actively packing (on paper) for 'A Trip to the Beach' by 'reading' a request from someone else to 'Let's Pack for a Trip.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying key information, and accurately transcribing words. Reinforcing the recognition and spelling of regularly spelled one- or two-syllable words related to the scenario (e.g., 'bucket,' 'sandy,' 'floaties').</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation of individual student lists and a quick check-in.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Beach Packing List" from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Student correctly identifies and lists at least 4 out of 5 items mentioned in the new text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student uses the correct vocabulary words for the items (e.g., 'towel,' 'sunscreen,' 'bucket,' 'flip-flops').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness / Legibility:</b> The words written on the list are legible and correctly spelled (within A1.2 expectations for regularly spelled words from the text).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket by circling the face that best describes their learning or answering simple questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about what to pack for the beach." (Yes / Almost / Not yet)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can make a list of things for the beach from a message." (Yes / Almost / Not yet)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new beach word did you read today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading strategies worked best for 4th-grade students (e.g., circling, underlining, matching)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully demonstrate comprehension of the simple written exchanges?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to create lists from the information read?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with decoding or vocabulary recognition? Perhaps more visual aids or repetition of phonemic blending.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 3, students will move from reading about packing for a trip to actively speaking and interacting about what to pack for the beach, using the same vocabulary and grammar structures in short dialogues and role-plays. This lesson's reading comprehension provides the necessary foundation for oral production.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>